A||||15||Richard Parnell||Dual Exhaust Question||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||05-12-2000||04:59 PM||||When I think of nice dual exhausts, I think of (On six and 8 cylinders) separate pipes and mufflers for each side of the engine all the way out to the tips.  Now I notice a lot of performance cars have their exhaust pipes join in a box or have a crossover before they spit out to their individual muffler. What is the theory behind this? I saw it on a Corvette the other day so it got me thinking (and that hurts).||||reg||5
Z||000001||Eric||05-12-2000||05:22 PM||||I think it's done for torque or emmisions.  To tell you the truth, I have no idea what it's for.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fiasco||05-12-2000||05:53 PM||||I believe the joined dual exhausts are that way for temperature equalization. The hotter pipe dissipates some heat at the junction to the cooler pipe.<BR>For example, if you are travelling north in a west wind, the pipe on the east side of the car is downwind and therefore does not get as much cool air flow around it. Without this junction of the two pipes, the downwind pipe will super-heat and start a fire and/or cause an explosion, depending on the location of the fuel tank.<BR>(ok I have no idea, either.)<p>[This message has been edited by Fiasco (edited 05-12-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000003||87PegasusGT||05-12-2000||06:14 PM||||Emissions...<BR>But I like Fiasco's explanation better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||05-12-2000||06:22 PM||||When I was a teenager my dad had a 64 Ford station wagon. It had a 390 4V with duals all the way out the back (ran like he!! for a SW). It also had a pipe running between the duals right around the transmission area. There must be SOME tuning/performance benefit to letting the separate exhaust get acquanted. I can't imagine they did ANYTHING for emissions reasons in 1964...<P>Then again... maybe Fiasco is on to something  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||05-12-2000||06:46 PM||||I think you will find that it is done to balance the back pressure between the cly banks||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||05-12-2000||06:53 PM||||depends how it's done. Done right it improves cylinder scavanging. Done wrong it can mess up the whole flow.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||05-12-2000||07:23 PM||||It's called a balance pipe and used for two reasons. A couple people mentioned (at least Phil did) that it balanced the back pressure between the two banks. This, if done correctly as Ogre points out, will help with improving flow and torque.<P>The second reason is that it evens out the noise pulses and actually will sound much quietier than the tradition duals. That's why single exhaust is used on all luxury cars. Smooth, quiet sounding exhaust. Some of the younger Forum members might not want quiet.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Richard Parnell||05-12-2000||07:47 PM||||So the V6 Fieros which actually exhaust to one muffler and then split out to dual tips, would have a different sound and performance qualities if they had a muffler for each side of the engine (each group of 3 cylinders).??||||reg||5
Z||000009||Spyder||05-12-2000||08:18 PM||||If you keep them seperate on the V6 and use 2 cherry bombs or 2 glass packs it would sound like a V12.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||05-12-2000||10:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Spyder:<BR><B>If you keep them seperate on the V6 and use 2 cherry bombs or 2 glass packs it would sound like a V12.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Works for me! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  What about no cat or mufflers, but a set of SuperTrapps??  I know one of the forum members has gone this way.  I just can't remember who.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Cooter||05-12-2000||10:43 PM||||The pipe to tie the duals together is a pressure balance pipe. On engines with an even (bank to bank) firing order, they are not a lot of benefit. But on a GM v8 that has two cylinders firing back to back on the same bank, it allows that extra pressure pulse to be equalized into the other pipe. On my Suburban, the cross over pipe really helped with mid-range resonance in the pipes. It really toned down the Flowmasters to a tolerable level.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||05-13-2000||10:08 AM||||So does the 2.8 have an even firing order?  What exactly would a true dual exhaust (no crossover) do for the 2.8?  How would it sound in comparison to the stock set up?||||reg||1
Z||000013||mwbackus||05-13-2000||04:31 PM||||One should consider the issue of the oxygen sensor before tearing the stock system out.<P>Mike B.||||reg||12
Z||000014||WKDFIRO||05-14-2000||02:56 PM||||The way it was explained to me was that since the V6 has an uneven number of cyl on each bank that the pulses need to be evened out for better flow and scavenging. Cross flow has proven to be advantageous in numerous magazines tests. Running a true duel exhaust is NOT better for sound on a V6. I have heard a 3800 with true dual exhaust. With the odd number of pulses you have at least one or more pulses at idle that becomes its own vacuum pulse or negative pulse that is uncoupled. This makes the a small pop noise when it exits the tail pipe and almost sounds like a misfire to the untrained ear. The first Vipers (5 cyls on each bank!) were having problems with people bringing in their new Vipers for what they thought was a misfire. When they went away from the side exhuast and went to the back pipes performance when up and supposedly the sound improved. How much you wanna bet they put a cross pipe in there. Hope this clears things up or at the very least didn't confuse people too badly.||||reg||2
Z||000015||1FieroGTRacingInc||05-14-2000||06:59 PM||||So how would the TRUE dual's with Monkeyman's supertrap's on the v6 sound or perform IF  you used a crosspipe???<BR>would it be different then our stock setup???||||reg||7
